<h2 id="california-rules">California and San Diego tax rules</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> California taxes self-employment profit at rates from 1% to 12.3%, plus an additional 1% on income above $1 million. Pay quarterly estimates to the Franchise Tax Board.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit, plus federal income tax.</li>
  <li><strong>AB5:</strong> California's ABC test makes it harder for businesses to classify workers as contractors. Keep your own tools, clients and schedule if you freelance. App-based drivers fall under Proposition 22.</li>
  <li><strong>City Business Tax Certificate:</strong> Required for home-based businesses and independent contractors working in the City of San Diego. The annual fee is $34 for 12 or fewer employees, with small additional one-time and state fees.</li>
  <li><strong>Short-term rentals:</strong> Hosting requires a Short-Term Residential Occupancy license, and whole-home licenses are capped. Hosts also owe transient occupancy tax.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> Sell physical products? Get a seller's permit from the California Department of Tax and Fee Administration.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Expect 1099 forms from apps and clients. Keep receipts for supplies, insurance and mileage, because California and federal taxes on unplanned profit add up fast.</div>

